#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b65e21-4124-599c-8ef5-38bd7add3afa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e257b16a-a775-5397-9d95-12edd8b09eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{'}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{'}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441ce2f1-8463-59ab-9df1-c1090a81dfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln5}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441ce2f1-8463-59ab-9df1-c1090a81dfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln5}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259777} :answer-field/id #uuid "68586d47-416c-5b02-a214-0b04cc3eb25e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807afca8-b2c0-59ad-99e9-bb1c9df82ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336927" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x]}^{'}}{[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}5)}{2\\operatorname{ln}5\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}5} & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1]}^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}5){5}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}5){e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
